      "side": 1,
      "message": "My hypothesis is it\u0027s hard to get 5 allocations at any one time given a random sequence of allocates and frees. I think we were getting lucky with the seed when compiling for 64-bits, but less lucky when compiling for 32-bits. I think the seed doesn\u0027t vary per-run to avoid flakes. Here\u0027s an approximation of how many allocs you can get when you alloc size rand % n, though it\u0027s not quite the same as the test since the test randomly does additional frees and allocs:\n\nhttps://paste.googleplex.com/6653229610827776\n$ python myrand.py\nMean: 1.802900 stddev: 0.919639\n\nSo if you do rand() % n allocs until you exhaust your heap of size n, on average you will get only 1.8 allocations. The heap size in both 32 and 64-bits seems to be 0x8680.",
